rce=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Shiseibyō","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.219167,"lo":127.671944,"d":1001,"ds":"The Shiseibyō (至聖廟) is a Confucian temple in the Wakasa district of Naha, Okinawa. It served for centuries as a major center of Chinese learning for the Ryūkyū Kingdom, and contains within its precincts the Meirindō, first public school in Okinawa.","wpu":"Shiseibyō","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Naha_Siseibyo03n2700.jpg/960px-Naha_Siseibyo03n2700.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fukushūen","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.21797,"lo":127.675943,"d":1263,"ds":"Fukushūen (福州園, lit. \"Fuzhou Garden\" or \"Foochow Garden\") is a traditional Chinese garden in the Kume area of Naha, Okinawa. The garden was constructed in 1992, in celebration of the 10th anniversary of the establishment of the sister city relationship between Naha and Fuzhou in China.","wpu":"Fukushūen","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Fukushuen_Garden.JPG/960px-Fukushuen_Garden.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sōgen-ji","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.220333,"lo":127.690583,"d":2223,"ds":"Sōgen-ji (Okinawan: 崇元寺) was a Buddhist temple located in the Tomari neighborhood of the city of Naha, Okinawa Prefecture, Japan. It belonged to the Rinzai school sect of Japanese Zen. The temple's full name is Reitoku-san Sōgen-ji (Okinawan: 霊徳山 崇元寺).","wpu":"Sōgen-ji","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/Sogenji_Stone_Gate.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Gusuku Sites and Related Properties of the Kingdom of Ryukyu","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.208611,"lo":127.682778,"d":2504,"ds":"The Gusuku Sites and Related Properties of the Kingdom of Ryukyu (琉球王国のグスク及び関連遺産群, Ryūkyū ōkoku no gusuku oyobi kanren'isangun) is a UNESCO World Heritage Site which consists of nine sites all located in the Okinawa Prefecture, Japan.","wpu":"Gusuku Sites and Related Properties of the Kingdom of Ryukyu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Naha_Shuri_Castle16s5s3200.jpg/960px-Naha_Shuri_Castle16s5s3200.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"National Theatre Okinawa","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.2464,"lo":127.688546,"d":2758,"ds":"National Theatre Okinawa (国立劇場おきなわ, Kokuritsu Gejō Okinawa) opened in Urasoe, Okinawa Prefecture, Japan, in January 2004. The main auditorium seats 632 and there is also a smaller hall with a capacity of 255.","wpu":"National Theatre Okinawa","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/National_Theater_Okinawa.jpg/960px-National_Theater_Okinawa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Yamashita First Cave Site Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.200833,"lo":127.672838,"d":3034,"ds":"Yamashita First Cave Site Park (山下洞穴遺跡公園, Yamashita Dōketsuiseki Kōen) is an urban park in Naha, Okinawa. The park was opened by the city in December 2015. An opening inauguration ceremony was held on January 8, 2016, in Yamashita Town, Naha.","wpu":"Yamashita First Cave Site Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Entryway_to_Yamashita_First_Cave_Site_Park_in_Naha_Okinawa_JA.jpg/960px-Entryway_to_Yamashita_First_Cave_Site_Park_in_Naha_Okinawa_JA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Okinawa Prefectural Library","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.208429,"lo":127.694182,"d":3248,"ds":"Okinawa Prefectural Library (沖縄県立図書館, Okinawa Kenritsu Toshokan) opened in Naha, Okinawa Prefecture, Japan in 1910. Iha Fuyū was the first director. The library reopened in a new building in 1983.","wpu":"Okinawa Prefectural Library","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/120214_Okinawa_Prefectural_Library_Naha_Okinawa_pref_Japan02s3.jpg/960px-120214_Okinawa_Prefectural_Library_Naha_Okinawa_pref_Japan02s3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ryukyu Kingdom","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.2,"lo":127.683333,"d":3385,"ds":"The Ryukyu Kingdom was a kingdom in the Ryukyu Islands from 1429 until 1872, when it was reorganized as the Ryukyu Domain, before being fully abolished in 1879. It was ruled as a tributary state of the Ming dynasty by the Ryukyuan monarchy, who unified Okinawa Island to end the Sanzan period, and subsequently extended the kingdom to the Amami Islands and Sakishima Islands.","wpu":"Ryukyu Kingdom","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Merchant_flag_of_the_Ryukyu_Kingdom.svg/960px-Merchant_flag_of_the_Ryukyu_Kingdom.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sueyoshi-gū","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":26.230167,"lo":127.714056,"d":4400,"ds":"Sueyoshi Shrine (末吉宮, Sueyoshi-gū; Okinawan: Shīshi-gū) is a Shinto shrine in the Shuri-Sueyoshi neighborhood of the city of Naha in Okinawa Prefecture, Japan. Its grounds were designated a National Historic Site in 1972. The main festival of the shrine is held annually on November 23.","wpu":"Sueyoshi-gū","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Sueyoshi_Park_Naha_Okinawa_Japan27s3.jpg/960px-Sueyoshi_Park_Naha_Okinawa_Japan27s3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Okinawa Island (Japanese: 沖縄島, Hepburn: Okinawa-jima; Okinawan: 沖縄 / うちなー, romanized: Uchinā; Kunigami: ふちなー, romanized: Fuchináa), also known as Okinawa Main Island (沖縄本島, Okinawa-hontō), is the largest of the Okinawa Islands and the Ryukyu (Nansei) Islands of Japan in the Kyushu region. The island is approximately 106 kilometres (66 mi) long, an average 11 kilometres (7 mi) wide, and has an area of 1,206.98 square kilometers (466.02 sq mi). It is roughly 640 kilometres (350 nmi; 400 mi) south of the main island of Kyushu and the rest of Japan.","wpu":"Okinawa Island","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Okinawa_Island-ISS042.jpg/960px-Okinawa_Island-ISS042.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}